WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.780
RACHEL TSAO: Hello, and welcome
to Top Use Cases to Start Your

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.730
Serverless Journey.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.040
My name is Rachel
Tsao, Product Manager

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.050
for Serverless at Google Cloud.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.890
Today, I'm joined by my
colleague Preston Holmes.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.020
We'll cover the
benefits and use cases

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.110
of using Google Cloud
serverless compute.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.920
We'll start with why modernizing
is beneficial for everyone,

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.930
whether you're an
established enterprise

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.120
or a fast-growing
digital native.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.670
We'll cover why Google
Cloud serverless.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.670
We'll dive into why
serverless is a good fit

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.860
for pragmatic enterprises.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.260
And then I will hand
it over to Preston

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.550
to talk about great workloads
for your serverless journey.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.800
Let's start with why modernize.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.660
As we look towards
the future, there

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.150
is a growing need for
companies to create value

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.010
through application design.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.910
Companies are looking to delight
customers with responsiveness,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.730
derive value from data,
and be insights driven,

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.040
respond faster to
change quickly,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.340
and be secure and available
in regions globally.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.790
They also want to be well-run
with effective cost management

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.650
while being able
to scale quickly

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.570
to potentially
millions of users.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.510
These characteristics
are no longer

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.140
just a choice, but an
expectation from customers.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.440
But companies face
barriers internally.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.110
These include people and
organizational barriers,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:38.250
like a lack of confidence in new
technology, a need for control,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.660
a lack of cloud-native
skills, a lack of automation.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.380
It can also be resistance
to change, [INAUDIBLE],, lack

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.530
of innovation, lack
of cost optimization,

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:53.610
and the need to comply
with security policies.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:56.940
Overcoming these barriers
with a cloud-native strategy

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.430
is important as
modern applications

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.000
are able to achieve
outstanding outcomes, including

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.270
being dynamic, scalable,
and intelligent.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.370
These characteristics
are increasingly

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:13.070
important for them to meet
fast-changing user needs.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.560
A cloud-native
strategy is possible,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.980
whether your company is
migrating from virtual machines

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.550
or building new
applications cloud native.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.890
When a company is
migrating from VMs,

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.150
they often face tight budgets,
security concerns, the need

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.460
to maintain legacy
infrastructure,

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.940
and the need to migrate
and modernize their APIs.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.020
Modernizing to serverless can
help meet many of these demands

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.290
in a way that is developer
friendly while considering

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.690
costs and time to market.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.300
Similarly, building
new applications

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.180
requires services to be
built in a way that reacts

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.370
to market and customer demands.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.190
For these new
applications, we developers

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.020
want to use the
latest technology.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.880
We don't want to
think about upgrades.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.700
We want to take advantage of
the new economics provided

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.270
by the cloud, and we want
architectural portability

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.380
so we are not locked in.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.080
No matter where
you are starting,

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.420
serverless is a great
choice and meets

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.900
demand of modern applications.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.620
Now, let's explore why
Google Cloud serverless.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.410
Serverless is a set
of characteristics.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.780
It simplifies the
developer experience

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.720
by eliminating the need
to manage infrastructure.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.120
It is scalable out of
the box, whether you

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.370
want to scale down
to 0 with no traffic,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.980
or up to millions of users.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.970
It has a pay-as-you-use model,
where you pay for exactly what

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.420
you use instead of guessing
the capacity upfront.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.410
If you don't have any traffic,
you don't pay for anything.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.720
Serverless technology enables
companies to quickly build,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.620
develop, and deploy any
application in a fully

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.430
automated environment.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:57.220
It removes the
operational burden

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.040
of infrastructure management
with built-in security,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.430
operations, and
DevOps best practices.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:05.410
As our customer
needs have evolved,

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.780
we've built our serverless
compute portfolio

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.180
to enable them in
their journeys.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.510
As many of you know, App Engine
was one of the first serverless

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.140
solutions to market in
2008, and early customers

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.779
took advantage of
serverless and App Engine

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:21.850
as they scaled their
web applications

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:25.650
across millions of users
on Google's infrastructure.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.890
We then added the ability to
run functions in the service

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.310
to our serverless
portfolio in 2017.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.550
With Cloud Functions, we give
customers a simple developer

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.880
experience with the ability
to rapidly write and run

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.070
tiny snippets of code.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:44.070
In parallel, Google also
introduced innovations

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.230
to the container
market with Kubernetes,

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.240
and we started to
hear from customers

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.370
who wanted to know if
we could combine the two

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.030
awesome serverless attributes
of autoscaling and developer

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.630
experience with the
flexibility of containers.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.050
In response, we introduced
Cloud Run, the next generation

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:01.980
of serverless.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.160
Serverless is no longer about
event-driven programming

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.960
or microservices.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.170
It's also about running
complex workloads at scale

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:13.780
while preserving a delightful
developer experience.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.720
In fact, serverless
with Cloud Run

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.410
is about having a true developer
platform with the flexibility

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.590
to run any language,
any library, any binary.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.480
Serverless offers the
following benefits.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.240
The infrastructure
is managed by Google,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.490
meaning that you
benefit from deploying

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.700
on the same infrastructure
as Google engineers.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.000
Your engineers do not need
to think or spend time

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.500
worrying about scale.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.680
Because you only pay for
when there is traffic,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.010
it often enables
great cost savings.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.410
And finally,
serverless is secure.

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.080
Google manages
infrastructure-level security

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.320
for you, ensuring that you
have the latest patches.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.700
You can choose from a suite of
Google Cloud security features

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.600
to meet your business needs.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.180
Google Cloud serverless
is the pragmatic choice

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.460
for enterprises,
and it's not just

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.240
for lightweight applications,
like this zippy golf cart

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.650
you might imagine here.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.700
We're actually enabling all
kinds of heavy duty workloads,

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:19.645
like this tractor.

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:27.650
In fact, this year, we unlocked
several security features

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.940
on Cloud Run, making it even
more secure for enterprises.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.150
With Binary Authorization
and Manage Secrets,

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.330
you can ensure your
application is safe to run.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:41.570
With Cloud Armor support,
identity-aware proxy,

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:44.240
ingress settings,
and IAM ingress,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.060
you can ensure the
right folks have access.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.430
And finally, you can understand
what your application can

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:55.090
access with restriction
egress out of VPC,

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.550
intelligent security
recommendations,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.440
and VPC-SC network
egress settings.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.950
We've also unlocked new
pricing options on Cloud Run.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.260
Out of the box, you
only pay for what you

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.840
use for under 200 milliseconds.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.890
And this year, we introduced
committed use discounts,

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.650
meaning that if you commit
to one or three years,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:19.840
you can save up to 17%.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.580
And finally, we also introduced
CPU allocation controls,

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:25.470
meaning that if you
have a workload that

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.130
needs to run continuously, we
do not charge for service fees

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:33.500
to start Cloud Run back
up every single time.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.900
In addition, all our
serverless products

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.440
offer built-in observability
and monitoring without setup.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.140
For example, requests
and container logs

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:45.320
are automatically sent to
Cloud Logging from Cloud Run.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.450
And error reporting
is automatically

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.190
enabled for Cloud Run.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:52.140
No additional setup or
configuration is required.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:54.800
You can simply view logs
on the Cloud Run page,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.310
and also in the Cloud
Logging console.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:00.200
Choosing Google
Cloud serverless can

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.720
help eliminate
operational overhead,

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.770
manage costs, all in
an innovative building

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.940
model with easier scalability.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.030
Now I'll hand it over
to Preston to see

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:13.828
what workloads are a great fit.

00:08:13.828 --> 00:08:15.120
PRESTON HOLMES: Thanks, Rachel.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.150
So let's talk about
some of these workloads.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.900
You'll note that in
Google Cloud platform,

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:23.080
we have a range of options
to run the compute portion

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.940
of your application code.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.680
And these can be
broadly laid out

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.570
on a spectrum between
convenience and control.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:32.530
So we've got Cloud Functions
for very small, lightweight

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:33.730
snippets of code.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:36.580
We've got serverless
containers with Cloud Run.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:38.770
We've got cluster-based
infrastructure

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:41.409
in Kubernetes and GKE.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:44.500
And then, for VMs, we've
got Google Compute Engine.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.680
So, many of these workloads
are potentially a better fit

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.120
for one of these than another.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.490
But rather than create
a large decision tree,

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:53.980
we've decided to pick out
three workloads that we think

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.220
are a particularly good fit
for serverless and Cloud Run,

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:02.230
and those are web applications,
data processing, and APIs.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.060
Now, I'll actually
speak to a little bit

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:08.680
of a sub flavor of each of these
to give it a bit more context

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.570
of where you can get started.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.250
And so, for web
applications, I'm

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:14.540
going to talk about
intranet web applications.

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:17.830
So these are applications
designed to be accessed only

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:20.020
by company employees.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.520
And what we want
to do is we want

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.590
to bring you a bit of
how we get to interact

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:26.410
with our internal applications
at Google, which Google

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:29.380
has published some research
about we call BeyondCorp.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:31.210
And that is, at
Google, we no longer

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:34.630
use VPNs to access
internal applications.

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:38.350
Instead, we use very secure
Identity Aware proxies

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:40.450
that can use context
about an employee

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:42.160
to make sure they
have access only

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.600
to the right internal
applications.

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.050
Now, we have externalized
this in Cloud platform

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.570
as the Identity
Aware proxy, and we

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.450
can put that in front of
our serverless Cloud Run

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:54.050
applications.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.040
The idea of IAP is
that we only want

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.610
authorized and corporate
employees to be able to access

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.140
those internal applications.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.400
And they should not be
accessible by the public.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.010
But at the same
time, we don't want

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.380
to burden our
internal developers

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:10.930
with having to
recreate and reproduce

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:14.500
a secure posture for each
new intranet application.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.840
Instead, that should
be globally handled

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:19.870
by a frontend system
that will make sure

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:23.380
only our employees are
accessing these applications.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:25.270
Now, this is
specifically delivered

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:27.820
through a composition and
integration of Google Cloud

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:29.410
platform products.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:33.190
It starts with Cloud Identity,
which provides an identity

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.790
service so that we know who it
is that's logging into our web

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:38.560
applications.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.070
It can then be guarded with
Cloud Armor, a web application

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:45.640
firewall that does things
like rate limiting, protection

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:50.230
of our applications, or can
whitelist certain IP ranges.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.840
We then hit the
Identity Aware proxy.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.230
This gives our users
that user login flow.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:58.750
So it makes sure
that every access

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.600
to the actual application
has been authorized

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.600
and only identified
users are logging in.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.150
And then, finally,
the load balancer

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:10.750
distributes these requests to
multiple different intranet

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:11.930
apps.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.800
So let's take a look at a
demo of this in practice

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.470
and see what it can look
like at your company.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.770
So here you can see
the intranet project

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:24.280
I have set up with several
example applications already

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:25.300
deployed.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.490
I have an Intranet Home app.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:30.430
This acts as the
home page for listing

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:31.870
all of my other applications.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:36.340
And then a Birthdays application
and a Vacations application.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.890
You'll note that the ingress
setting for all of these

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:41.410
is set to internal
and load balancing.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:45.430
What that means is that these
are not reachable directly

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:46.210
from the internet.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:48.730
You can see, while this URL
is here, it's grayed out

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.070
and is not directly accessible.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:54.760
This is actually enforced
for any new Cloud Run

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:58.190
application deployed
through organization policy.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:00.760
So for the entire project or
maybe a folder of projects,

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.570
I can set that the only allowed
values for this ingress setting

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:09.110
is internal traffic only, or
internal plus load balancing.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:10.840
So we are running
these applications

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:12.310
behind a load
balancer, and we have

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:14.780
two backend services set up.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:17.770
The first is really just
used as our catchall to route

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:20.080
to that intranet home page.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:25.370
The other is using a wild
card pattern in the host rule.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.550
And what that wild
card pattern does

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:30.760
is allow us to use a
single mask applications

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.590
network endpoint group.

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.570
This network
endpoint group takes

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:38.050
advantage of a feature with
its own URL mask there.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:41.320
And this service
placeholder in the URL mask

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.950
means that for every new
intranet application I

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:47.950
deploy that has a service
name, I automatically

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:51.730
have the route set up to reach
that particular Cloud Run

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:53.920
service in the
single load balancer

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:56.920
without needing
to manually create

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:01.810
each additional backend for
each new intranet application.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.030
Now, the BeyondCorp
part of this is

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:07.750
that I run these behind
the Identity Aware proxy.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:12.070
This will force the login flow
to reach these applications.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:15.610
I actually have this set
up to use our organization

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.000
only as a source of identity.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:22.020
So this is limited just
to our organization.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:26.120
The last thing we'll do here is
just check on public DNS Google

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:29.030
to see that the domain name
I have set up with my load

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.590
balancer is resolving.

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:31.550
And it does.

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:33.470
It resolves to this
IP address, which

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:38.100
is the same as that set up
in the load balancer here.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.100
So with that, let's
take a look at what

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:41.700
the login flow looks like.

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.015
Again, this will be the
first-time login for a user.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:50.780
And if I do that, I'm given
a standard login dialog here.

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:53.165
This is using, in this
case, my Google account.

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:57.920
But we could also use a
corporate SSO flow for IAP.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.010
And now I'm on the
intranet home page.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.620
Now, I'm seeing a couple
other of the applications

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:04.380
I can get to.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.920
So for example, I can
schedule some time off.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:10.640
This will route me to
the Vacations service

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:15.680
that has got its own hostname
path behind this load balancer.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.480
I could also go to
the Birthday service.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.320
And again, this will route
to that individual Cloud Run

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:21.830
service.

00:14:21.830 --> 00:14:23.750
Each of these can be
independently managed

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:26.840
and deployed by different
groups, or teams, or authors.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.920
And in fact, you see that IAP
will pass through information

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:32.100
about the logged in user.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:36.080
So applications can make use of
a already authenticated value

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.480
for the current
user to do things

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:41.290
like user-specific
profiles or updates,

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:45.410
or further rule-based
authorization.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:48.020
Now, subsequent logins
to this will not

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:51.800
require any particular
auth flow, except subject

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.820
to sort of any timeouts policy
my org has set up because this

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:58.190
is using cookies to
remember this login

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:00.110
for some period of time.

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:05.450
If I try to log in as a user
from some non-organization user

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:08.060
account, you'll see
that I don't even

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:14.810
have the option of logging
in because the entire auth

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.760
application is limited to
only organization users.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.870
So this will be another
layer of defense

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:23.600
that prevents anyone from
accidentally enabling

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.580
specific permission to a user.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:30.410
That's an option on IAP that
you can set up and either allow

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:31.530
that or not allow that.

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:33.000
So you're not forced to do that.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.750
But if you do want to keep
this as an organization-only

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.550
intranet setup, you can do that.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:40.820
And so deploying
a new intranet app

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:42.530
is pretty much
just as easy as now

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.900
deploying another
container-based Cloud Run

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:45.800
service.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:49.940
Each of these scales down
to 0 and makes it very easy.

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.310
Great, now that we've taken
a look at internet web

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:55.700
applications, let's take
a look at data processing.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:58.700
Now, data processing is really
a catchall or umbrella term

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:02.240
that might cover any number of
types of changes or mutations

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:03.990
you're going to make
to the data you have,

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:07.400
which include converting
data, packaging data,

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:08.880
validating data.

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:11.725
So it really covers a
wide range of use cases.

00:16:11.725 --> 00:16:13.100
So let's take a
look at something

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.600
that's a bit more
specific, which

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.330
is performing some
lightweight data processing

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:18.980
at the time of ingest.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:21.230
Now, on Cloud Platform,
we have a product

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:23.150
called Cloud Pub/Sub,
which is really

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:26.420
great at delivering messages
to many of our backend data

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:27.980
processing systems.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:30.200
And as an architectural
component,

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:33.440
it's often very useful to
decouple the producers of data

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.690
from those systems
which will consume it.

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:38.570
But there are times that
you need to bring data

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:39.590
into your platform.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:42.290
This might be video
game telemetry.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:43.520
Might be device data.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:46.130
It might be some sort
of external log ingest

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:49.310
where what you want to do is
not completely decouple this

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:50.300
at the point of ingest.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.242
So you might want to provide
custom authentication

00:16:53.242 --> 00:16:54.200
at the point of ingest.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.900
Or you might want to validate
the contents of each message

00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:00.680
and make sure that only good
and verified data is making it

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:02.450
onto the Pub/Sub topic.

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:06.109
And so Cloud Run acts as a very
nice, scalable point of ingest

00:17:06.109 --> 00:17:09.230
where you can both
receive the data into GCP,

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.020
as well as perform some
lightweight data processing

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:14.060
at that point of ingest.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:16.280
Another one of the use
cases we want to talk today

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:18.410
is about REST APIs.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:20.888
Now, REST APIs are not
a brand new use case.

00:17:20.888 --> 00:17:22.430
In fact, they've
been around a while.

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:24.305
But they still remain
a real bread and butter

00:17:24.305 --> 00:17:27.650
of many enterprise IT projects.

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:30.590
This is often where you
have multiple surfaces

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:34.820
in the backend composed into
a single API service, where

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:38.090
each backend microservice
might be only contributing

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:40.890
one element of an
overall API experience.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.880
So in this case, we have a
hypothetical weather service

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:47.090
where you might have
alerts, forecast, locations,

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:50.120
each being fulfilled
by a separate backend

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:52.580
and independently
deployed microservice.

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.590
Now, these are all
routed through some sort

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:58.130
of API management
system, often serving

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.710
the need of authentication,
or rate limiting,

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:02.330
or quota management.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:06.140
And then these reach the end
user application and either

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.010
a client or a mobile
web application.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:13.850
One of the customers using Cloud
Run in this case is ecobee.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:17.530
ecobee uses Cloud Run for API
services, driving experience,

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:20.010
and functionality in
their customer-facing web

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:21.870
and mobile applications.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:23.550
This has allowed
engineering teams

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:25.560
to ship new features
to their customers

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:29.040
faster, as new services
can be turned up in days,

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.950
and iteration is made faster
by using the same serverless

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.460
technologies to simplify QA
environments, streamlining

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:38.180
the path from code
to production.

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:40.580
By using fully managed
databases together

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:42.830
with serverless
compute, services

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:46.070
scale smoothly and
reliably as a whole.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:49.430
These technologies have led to
greater operational efficiency,

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:51.230
removing many of
the incident pages

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:54.260
which were due purely to
infrastructure issues instead

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.713
of the applications themselves.

00:18:56.713 --> 00:18:58.130
And with that, I
want to thank you

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:01.690
on behalf of Rachel and
myself for joining us today.

